Gateway creation and reassignment authority
==========================================

Creating or editing a saved gateway must validate its destination against the
current database. The organization must be active and not deleted. An assigned
site must be active, not deleted, and belong to that organization. Existing
caller site restrictions also apply. An invalid destination produces the fixed
404 message "Gateway not found" through the standard API error envelope.

Destination checks use PostgreSQL SHARE row locks until the request transaction
ends. KEY SHARE alone is insufficient because changing an active/deleted flag
does not change the parent's key. A parent writer already in progress must
finish before the assignment proceeds; a newly invalid parent is then refused.
A parent status change begun after validation waits for assignment commit.

An update additionally selects and refreshes the current gateway under a row
lock before applying local fields or merging encrypted credentials. The initial
gateway lookup, current organization/site predicate, host validation and role
permissions remain enforced. No vendor adapter is constructed by these save
operations. Registry dispatch and adapter eviction keep their existing behavior.

Compatibility
-------------
- An unrestricted caller may create an unassigned organization-wide gateway.
- A caller restricted to specific sites must supply a site when creating; the
  existing required-site 422 remains unchanged.
- A missing or explicitly null site in an update still means no site change.
- The application's existing zero-explicit-grant organization-wide policy is
  unchanged; this change does not introduce a new role or grant model.
- Credentials remain encrypted and absent from the save response.

Qualification scope and remaining limits
---------------------------------------
The added tests use real committed PostgreSQL rows with synthetic caller identity
and dispatch counters. They cover invalid destinations, inactive organizations,
stale ORM site records, allowed saves, denied roles and transaction lock lifetime.
Separate TCP tests use real HTTP listeners and independent parent writers,
including a writer held open while a save attempts to acquire its parent lock.
These checks do not establish hardware interoperability or complete production
acceptance. Frozen source, full-suite and runtime qualification are recorded
separately; local focused passes must not be presented as shipping passes.

Caller role, session, API-key and site-grant revocation during a request remains
a separate concern. Registry notification is not made transactional by this
change. Reads and vendor operations already in flight are not recalled, and a
parent can legitimately become inactive after the assignment transaction ends.
No managed backup storage, restore, distributed cache invalidation, HA or
availability/latency guarantee is added.
